We shall prove by induction that  $a_{n}=na_1$  and therefore  $(a_n)$  is an arithmetic progression with common difference  $a_1$ .

Indeed, it is simple to check  $a_2=2a_1,a_3=3a_1$  etc, let us suppose  $a_n=na_1$ .
Now we are given 
\begin{align*} \sum_{i=1}^{n+1} a_i\binom{n+1}{i}& =2^{n}a_{n+1}
  \sum_{i=1}^{n} ia_1\binom{n+1}{i}& =(2^{n}-1)a_{n+1}
 a_1\sum_{i=1}^{n} i\binom{n+1}{i}& =(2^{n}-1)a_{n+1}
\end{align*}
And therefore  $a_{n+1}=(n+1)a_1\iff \sum_{i=1}^{n} i\binom{n+1}{i}=(2^n-1)(n+1)$  or equivalently iff  $S=\sum_{i=1}^{n+1} i\binom{n+1}{i}=2^n(n+1)$ .

Note that  $S=\sum_{i=1}^{n+1} i\binom{n+1}{i}=\sum_{i=1}^{n+1} i\binom{n+1}{n+1-i}$  and therefore: $2S=\sum_{i=1}^{n+1} i\binom{n+1}{i}+\sum_{i=1}^{n+1} i\binom{n+1}{n+1-i}=(n+1)\sum_{i=1}^{n+1}\binom{n+1}{i}=(n+1)(2^n)$  

We conclude  $S=2^{n-1}(n+1)$  and this means the induction is complete.

