Let  $L:=\lim_{x\to\infty}f(x)$  also, we'll be using the fact that  $f$  is bounded throughout without explicitly mentioning it each time. Making the obvious substition  $t=x/n$  we get 
	\begin{align}
\cfrac{1}{n}\int_{0}^{n}f(x)g\Big(\cfrac{x}{n}\Big)\, dx &=\int_{0}^{1}f(nt)g(t)\, dt. 
\end{align}
 Let  $0<\delta<1$ , pick a sufficiently large natural  $n_{\delta}$  such that for any  $n\ge n_\delta$  it follows,  $\displaystyle\sup_{\delta \le t\le 1}||f(nt)-L||\le \frac{\varepsilon}{\int_{0}^{1}|g(t)|\, dt}$  for an arbitrarily small  $\varepsilon>0$  (assuming  $g$  is not zero everywhere). From  $(1)$  we have
	\begin{align*}
	\Bigg\lvert\int_{\delta}^{1}g(t)(f(n_{\delta}t)-L)\, dt\Bigg\lvert &\le \int_{\delta}^{1}|g(t)(f(n_{\delta}t)-L)|\, dt
		&\le \sup_{\delta\le t\le 1}||f(n_{\delta}t)-L||\int_{0}^{1}|g(t)|\, dt
		&\le \varepsilon.
	\end{align*}
	Cleverly choosing a sequence of  $\delta$ 's that converges to  $0$ , it is necessary that
	\begin{align*}
		\underbrace{\lim_{\delta\to 0^+}\int_{0}^{\delta}f(n_{\delta}t)g(t)\, dt}_{\text{ $=0$ }}+\lim_{\delta\to 0^+}\int_{\delta}^{1}f(n_{\delta}t)g(t)\, dt &=\lim_{\delta\to 0^+}\int_{0}^{1}f(n_{\delta}t)g(t)\, dt
		&=\lim_{n\to\infty}\int_{0}^{1}f(nt)g(t)\, dt
                &=L\int_0^1 g(t)\, dt.
	\end{align*}
	Which is indeed the required.  $\square$

